A||||12||Sootah||Running with a thrown rod?||||3||
Z||000000||Sootah||10-14-2001||11:24 PM||||Is it feasible to plug up the hole of the exit wound from the rod and just run it on 5 cyls? This may be a stupid question as she smokes like a *****  after she's been running for a while (I think the rod exiting gave me a coolant leak) is it feasible? Not like it matteres if anything happens to this block now anyway. Don't have the funds for a 3.4 block or to have my other 2.8 rebuilt.<P>If this truely is a dumb question, then let the making fun of begin. Thanx,<P><I><B>-ThrewARodAndNotVeryHappyAboutIt</B></I><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sootah__Nuclear_Happy_Face_Sig.jpg">     <BR><sup><I>Never pull your pants down in Cosco, the dang monkeys will getcha</I></sup> <BR><sup><B>AIM</B>: KSSouter</sup>||||reg||3
Z||000001||California Kid||10-14-2001||11:33 PM||||Risks of running in priority:<BR>1. If the engine locks up while you under way, you may seriously damage the trans.<P>2. You may contaminate the entire cooling system and have to flush it out numerous times to get the oil out of it.<P>3. Better have the number of a tow company in your wallet, cause you'll most likely get stranded somewhere with a completely dead car.<P>Just my opinion.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Sootah||10-14-2001||11:37 PM||||How would this contaminate the cooling system? I was thinking that the actual rod exiting may have punctured a coolant line, as it didn't have a leak until I threw it. If you think that this will totally strand me then I am not gonna drive it about.<P>How much will a 3.4 short block in good condition cost me? And if it's FWD then I can just bolt everything up, can't I (including starter)?||||reg||3
Z||000003||Mach10||10-15-2001||12:16 AM||||Unfortunately, you'll have no oil pressure, and the crank will be seriously out of balance. Not only will this make the block shake like a sum'***** , but it will also kill the rest of the bearings/rods in a matter of hours. Sorry to say, the motor's toast.<P>You mentioned that you have a block in the back yard, with the oil cap off. Here's what you do:<P>Drag it into the garage, and pull the heads off. Check if there's any water in the cylinders. If not, then let the thing swim in WD-40 for a couple of hours (fill up the cylinders)<BR>Then, with a breakerbar on the crank (flywheel, whatever) rock it back and forth until it moves. Pour some real oil into the cylinders. Drop the pan off, and see if there's water in the oil. If not, then just clean any and all visible surfaces. The bottom end should hold together for a while. Longer than the 5-cyl will, anyway...<P>Pull your motor out, and swap blocks. Easy as that. <P>You only have to worry if there's water floating in the oil pan, or in a cylinder. Bearings/rings/cylinder walls don't like that. If there is, lug the works to a machine shop, and have the bottom-end rebuilt, and the cylinders honed. Deck your old heads, and have the valves freshened up. If you do all the work (minus machining) it shouldn't cost you more than about $500 at the most, assuming that the internals are ok. Now you have an almost fresh motor. <P>Good luck!<p>[This message has been edited by Mach10 (edited 10-15-2001).]||||reg||7
Z||000004||My7Fieros||10-15-2001||12:17 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>"If this is truely a dumb question, then let the making fun of begin."<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR><I>........nahhhh...too easy</I>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>Simple, you dont "plug up the hole" where the dead cyl is/was....You dont drive a car w/a thrown rod...PERIOD. What are you thinking?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/My7logo_1.gif"> ||||reg||1
